// Book Three, Chapter 16 (session 36). The autumn of 1792 told, and the night of the twentieth to the twenty first of January 1793. The Temple, Étienne's bed, the rue des Prouvaires, the third cellar, and the keys of the house.
// Opens on the autumn (no phase), then one night phase of three actions out of four, then the twenty first told at dusk, then a fixed closing scene in the long room that every player plays.
// Ends with rest() and => next. Reads b3.valere, b3.hunt, b3.senate, b3.baptiste, b3.abbaye, b3.carmes, b3.priest, b3.crossing, b3.slate, b3.vault, b3.aubry_post and b3.journal. Chapter 17 should read hunger, standing and secrecy as this chapter left them.
@chapter b3c16
@title The Winter of the Trial
@book crown-of-ashes
@version 1

# start
## The autumn
$ b3.temple = "left"
$ b3.etienne = "left"
$ b3.envoy = "heard"
$ b3.third = "left"
$ b3.keeper = "none"
~> autumn
?if must_feed()
  ~> hunger
?end
---
$ do phase("night", 3)
It is the night of the twentieth of January, a Sunday, a little after seven, and there is a hard frost. There are four things that want doing by your own reckoning, and time for three.
-> night

# autumn
The Prussians went home. It is a thing that is very hard to explain, and nobody has tried. On the twentieth of September, on a hill at Valmy, in a mist, a duke who had been told that it would be a promenade stood and looked at a French army that would not run, and at the end of an afternoon of cannon in which almost nobody was killed, he turned round. It was said at the time, by a man who was there, that from this day and this place there began a new era in the history of the world. It is quite possible that it was said by a poet.
On the twenty second the Convention proclaimed that the monarchy was no more, and that it was the first year of the Republic, and a great many persons who had signed their letters, a month before, with a very long list of titles, signed them with their surnames. The calendar has been altered. The saints have been taken out of it. The Seine has risen, and gone down. There are very few priests.
?if b3.abbaye == "spoke"
  A lieutenant of the Swiss, who is not now a lieutenant, and has not been a Swiss for some while, comes to the shop, in November, in a round hat, with a carter's whip, and stands at the counter for a quarter of an hour, with the face of a person who has come to a great house on a small errand, and goes out without having bought anything. He leaves a very small drawing, of a stag, on the counter. It is signed with an initial.
?elif b3.abbaye == "took"
  There is a letter from Basel, in November, in a hand that has been taught to write by somebody else. It does not say who it is from. It says only that the writer is well, and that the bread is white.
?else
  Nobody says the name. It is in the house, for a good while, like a stain on a ceiling.
?end
?if b3.carmes == "cadets"
  The four of the Cadets are in the quarry, under the shop. Clemence has learned, from an old compositor, to set type. She does it in the dark, by touch, very fast, with a face like a nun.
?elif b3.carmes == "priests"
  There are two of them left. They do not speak of the others. Clemence has put a small cross, in chalk, on the wall of the gallery, and rubbed it out, and put it again.
?else
  There are two of them left. Clemence has not spoken to you, except on the business of the house, since the Sunday. It is perfectly civil. It is a quite remarkable thing to have to bear.
?end
?if b3.crossing == "left"
  The Prince has not come back over the river. Ottaviano comes twice a month, at the edge of the dusk, with a card, and a bad temper, and goes away.
?else
  The Prince is in a house in the Marais, in the rue Saint-Antoine, with the blinds down, and a cook. He is said to be reading. He has not been to the Hôtel de Saint-Aubin since the Sunday.
?end
Étienne has not been downstairs since the new year. The old doctor from the rue des Cordeliers no longer comes. He has been replaced by a younger one, from the Section, with a cockade, who does not say it is nothing. Adélaïde has put the bed by the window, so that he may see the yard.
On the eleventh of December they brought the king before the Convention, to be tried. He was called Citizen Capet. In November a locksmith had told the Minister that there was an iron chest in a wall of the Tuileries, and the chest had been opened, and the papers in it had a great deal to say about a king who had taken the money of the nation and written to the emperor. It was thought to be rather conclusive. On the sixteenth, and the seventeenth, they voted. They voted by name, from the tribune, with the galleries shouting. It was death, by a majority of one, or of twenty six, or of fifty, depending on how you count the ones who said death with a condition, and the whole of Paris has counted it a different way. A motion for a reprieve was defeated on the nineteenth. It is to be done on the Monday.
