# morning
## The twenty first
You are in the haven before the sun, with the stone over you, and you hear it, as you have heard every great day of this century, only as a noise through the rock. It is not much of a noise. It is a drum. It is a great many drums, a long way off, beating all together, from a little after eight, and then, for a very short time, at about a quarter past ten, they stop. The silence is quite exact. It goes on for the space of a long breath, and then there is a roar, quite far away, that is not at all like a cheer. It goes on for rather a long time.
It is told you at dusk, by Aubry, who comes down the stair himself, in his brown coat, with his hat in his hand, and the lurcher at his heel, and stands at the foot, and does not at once speak.
He says that it was done at about twenty past ten, in the Place Louis Quinze, which is now called the Place of the Revolution, in a fog. He says that the whole of the Place was full, and that the National Guard stood in lines, with the cannon, and that there was nobody at the windows. He says that the king came in the mayor's carriage, with the priest, and got down, quite quickly, and took off his coat. He says that the king would have spoken, and that Santerre, on his horse, ordered the drums, and that the drums rolled, and that nobody heard what was said, and that it is not now to be known. He says that he has been told that it was a few words, about his innocence and the nation. He says that it was over in a minute. He says that the executioner held up the head, and the Place shouted, and that a man near him in the ranks began to laugh, and could not stop. He says that boys were selling handkerchiefs, soaked, afterward, at a sou. He says that he does not know what to think. He says that it is the first time in his life that he has not.
?if b3.aubry_post == "kept"
  He sits down, quite suddenly, on the bottom step, with the dog's head on his knee.
?elif b3.aubry_post == "sent"
  He has come on leave, from Soissons, for the week, and says that nobody in the camp spoke of it all day.
?end
?if b3.temple == "offered"
  He says that the sergeant of the Temple guard was seen, in the afternoon, very drunk, in the rue du Temple, shouting that the man had refused a rescue. He was arrested. Nobody has said for what.
?end
?if b3.journal == "backed"
  Baptiste has printed the account of the day, under the plate of the house, in four lines, and no comment. It is the only account in Paris that does not have an opinion in it. It has sold eleven thousand.
?elif b3.baptiste == "went"
  Baptiste was in the Place. He has said that he was there, and he has not said anything else.
?end
It is nine. The frost has not given.
-> keys

# keys
## The keys
He dies in the early part of the next night, a little after one, with the shutters open on the yard, quite easily, with his hand on the counterpane. It is not a thing that is expected to be exciting, and it is not. Adélaïde is at the bedside. Baptiste is on the other side, with his hat still on, and with the look of a person who has had too much of the day. The lamp is low. You are by the door.
?if b3.etienne == "told"
  He has said, an hour before, to you, without opening his eyes, that the sums came out.
?elif b3.etienne == "sat"
  He has said nothing for an hour. He has kept his fingers on the counterpane, very still, as one who has taken up a position.
?else
  You had not meant to be here. You came in at midnight, in the frost, by the yard door, and stood. It is a thing that you do not at once explain to yourself.
?end
?if b3.carmes == "cadets"
  Clemence has come up out of the quarry, and stands behind the door.
?end
It is a quiet death. It is a rather beautiful old face. He says that it has been a very good press, and that he is not sure that it was ever the right sort of paper, and that it is a pity about the cold. After that he does not say anything.
Adélaïde does not weep. She closes his eyes, and folds his hands, and straightens the nightcap, and says that there is a thing to be done. She takes a ring of keys out of his waistcoat pocket, where he kept them, which is the keys of the cellars, and the strongbox, and the old Swiss press, and the small door in the wall of the gallery that she has not been told of, and holds them in her palm. She does not look at them. She looks at you, and at Baptiste, and she says that it is not a thing that should wait till morning.
She says that the house has always had a keeper. She says that it is a name that is passed on, and that it was her father's, and his father's. She says that she would like to know who it is to be.
:the_keys
* Put the keys into her hand, and say that she is the keeper
  $ b3.keeper = "adelaide"
  $ standing += 2
  $ humanity += 2
  $ bond.adelaide += 3
  $ bond.baptiste -= 1
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_keepers_of_paris")
  You close her fingers on them. You say it in the old form, the one that Rome had for it, and that Florence had, and that was used at the shrine, and she says it after you, word for word, in a small hard voice. She is thirty three. She is a widow. She has kept the books for fourteen years. She says that it is exactly what she supposed. Baptiste has turned his face to the wall. He does not say a thing. He says, after a time, that he is glad.
* Give them to Baptiste, and say that the house is the press
  $ b3.keeper = "baptiste"
  $ defiance += 1
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
  $ bond.baptiste += 3
  $ bond.adelaide -= 2
  $ do chart("paris.presses")
  $ do discover("the_keepers_of_paris")
  He takes them as a man takes a hot dish. He has a very white face. He says, in a rough voice, that he is not able to keep books, and that he would be a good deal worse at keeping a secret, and that she knows it. Adélaïde looks at you, a long moment, with her hand still open. Then she shuts it. She says, in a perfectly level voice, that she will see to the funeral.
* Say that the house has been kept long enough, and let it close
  $ b3.keeper = "none"
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ cunning_brutal += 2
  $ humanity -= 3
  $ standing -= 1
  $ bond.adelaide -= 3
  $ bond.baptiste -= 3
  $ do chart("paris.counting")
  $ do discover("the_keepers_of_paris")
  It is the coldest thing that you have said in the whole of the century. You say that it is a time in which a house with such a secret is a danger to everyone in it. You say that the old name is a thing that has been carried by too many, for too long, and that there is no reason for her to carry it into a year like this one. You say that you will see that she is provided for. She listens, with the keys in her hand. She has gone quite gray. She says that it is very kind. She says that she did not know until now that it was possible to be so thoroughly dismissed.
It is a very long night. In the morning the frost has broken, and there is a rain, and the bells that have not been melted are all, for once, silent.
$ do rest()
